title: Romans 9
description: Romans 9 read in the context of Israel: God showing mercy to the nations through the gospel and hardening unbelieving Israel, not some humans elected to heaven and some to hell.
output: lessons/heresies/romans-9.html

# Source file named heresies-romans-9.deck because lessons/src/romans-9.deck is the Romans class deck.
# From the notes (studies/data/notes/romans-9-notes.html), in the author's words. The KJV passages the notes quote are
# pulled by the builder. One KJV wording fix: the question of 9:14 is given as the KJV has it ("unrighteousness").

=== Paul has been talking about Israel
--- Romans 9 [title]
overline: Answering error
heading: Romans 9
sub: Paul has been talking about Israel.
hint: Use the arrow keys or swipe to move through the slides. Tap any gold reference to read the verse.

--- Mercy to the nations, hardening of Israel
lead: He has just said God has the right to show mercy to whom He wills and to harden whom He wills.
p: In context that is not “some random humans elected to heaven, some elected to hell.” It is God showing mercy to the nations through the gospel and hardening unbelieving Israel—the same Israel that had the covenants, the law, the promises, and still stumbled over the Messiah:

--- They stumbled at that stumblingstone
scripture: Romans 9:30-33 | mark: Because they sought it not by faith

=== The question of 9:14
--- Is there unrighteousness with God?
p: That is why the question in [[Romans 9:14|9:14]] is “Is there unrighteousness with God?” It is the covenant people asking why God would treat them the way He treated Pharaoh: raise them up, harden them in their rebellion, and use that hardening to make His name known among the Gentiles.

--- Romans 9:14-18
scripture: Romans 9:14-18

=== The objector of 9:19
--- Who resists His will?
lead: So the objector in [[Romans 9:19|9:19]] is not a reprobate crying, “I never had a chance.”
p: It is a self-righteous Jew crying, “How can God find fault with us? We are the chosen nation. Who resists His will?”

--- Romans 9:19-21
scripture: Romans 9:19-21

--- That is not how the story is supposed to go
lead: In other words: “God promised us. He cannot set us aside and graft in those dirty Gentiles. That is not how the story is supposed to go.”

=== The potter
--- Paul answers with the potter
lead: That is why Paul answers with the potter. He is not quoting a philosophy textbook on compatibilism.
p: He is echoing Jeremiah 18. In Jeremiah the clay is a nation, and the potter remolds it in response to whether that nation turns.
callout: The point is not “the pot has no say.” The point is “the Potter has the right to do something you did not expect with a vessel that refused to repent.”

--- Jeremiah 18:6-10
scripture: Jeremiah 18:6-10 | mark: If that nation, against whom I have pronounced, turn from their evil

=== Keep reading
--- Keep reading and the Calvinist objector disappears
point: 9:30–32 | Gentiles obtained righteousness by faith; Israel did not because they pursued it by works.
point: 10:21 | “All day long I have stretched forth my hands unto a disobedient and gainsaying people.” That is not a people God never wanted.
point: 11:11 | They stumbled so that salvation would come to the Gentiles—to make Israel jealous.
point: 11:20–23 | They were broken off because of unbelief. If they do not continue in unbelief, they can be grafted in again.

--- Righteousness by faith, not by works
kicker: Romans 9:30–32
p: Gentiles obtained righteousness by faith; Israel did not because they pursued it by works.
scripture: Romans 9:30-32

--- Not a people God never wanted
kicker: Romans 10:21
big: Romans 10:21 | mark: All day long I have stretched forth my hands
p: That is not a people God never wanted.

--- To provoke them to jealousy
kicker: Romans 11:11
p: They stumbled so that salvation would come to the Gentiles—to make Israel jealous.
big: Romans 11:11

--- Broken off because of unbelief
kicker: Romans 11:20–23
p: They were broken off because of unbelief. If they do not continue in unbelief, they can be grafted in again.
scripture: Romans 11:20-23 | mark: because of unbelief they were broken off; if they abide not still in unbelief, shall be graffed in

=== Contingent on faith
--- Not stuck in a decree of reprobation
lead: The same “hardened” people are not stuck in a decree of reprobation. Their condition is contingent on faith.
p: If the objector were a man damned by an eternal decree he could never resist, Paul’s answer in chapter 11 would be cruel nonsense. You do not tell a vessel prepared for destruction, “Stop persisting in unbelief and you will be grafted back in.”

--- Quietly changing the subject
p: So the Calvinist has to treat [[Romans 9:19-23|9:19–23]] as a self-contained philosophy of individual election and then quietly change the subject when Paul keeps talking about Israel, unbelief, jealousy, and restoration.
p: The objector Paul actually answers is the one standing in the story: the covenant member who thinks God’s mercy to the nations and His judicial hardening of unbelieving Israel is unfair.

=== Close
--- The argument of Romans 9–11 [title]
heading: The argument of Romans 9–11
center: That objection makes sense in the argument of Romans 9–11. The “why did God create me to be damned” objection does not.
center: Paul is not defending God against the charge of authoring unbelief. He is defending God’s right to use a rebellious nation’s unbelief to save the world—and then invite that same nation back if they will believe.
